# XSH popen()
# Copyright (c) 2026 Nicholas Martin. All rights reserved.
#
# pclose() is used to end every stream (its own module is xsh/pclose). The
# commands use only POSIX shell syntax and standard utilities (cat, echo,
# test, true, sleep). Tests that could wait on a child that never ends are
# guarded by an alarm that fails the test.

assertion  XSH.popen.001
anchor     functions/popen.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/popen.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
since      1988
says       popen(command, "r") returns a stream from which the command's
           standard output can be read: for "x=ab; echo ${x}c" the stream
           yields "abc\n" and then end-of-file (the command is run by the
           shell language).
test       t/001-read.c

assertion  XSH.popen.002
anchor     functions/popen.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/popen.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
since      1988
says       popen(command, "w") returns a stream whose data becomes the
           command's standard input: "cat > out" ends up with the bytes
           written once pclose() has returned.
test       t/002-write.c

assertion  XSH.popen.003
anchor     functions/popen.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      1988
says       With mode "r" the command's standard input is the caller's
           (descriptor 0, redirected here to a file): "cat" yields the
           file's contents on the stream.
test       t/003-read-stdin.c

assertion  XSH.popen.004
anchor     functions/popen.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      1988
says       With mode "w" the command's standard output is the caller's
           (descriptor 1, redirected here to a file) and its standard
           error is the caller's descriptor 2.
test       t/004-write-stdout.c

assertion  XSH.popen.005
anchor     functions/popen.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      1988
says       With mode "r", the descriptor fileno(stream) is the readable end
           of the pipe: read() on it returns the command's output.
note       Whether the descriptor is also open for writing is unspecified
           (the pipe() page), so write() on it is not tried.
test       t/005-read-fileno.c

assertion  XSH.popen.006
anchor     functions/popen.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      1988
says       With mode "w", fileno(stream) is the writable end of the pipe:
           write() on it delivers bytes to the command ("cat > out").
note       Whether the descriptor is also open for reading is unspecified
           (the pipe() page), so read() on it is not tried.
test       t/006-write-fileno.c

assertion  XSH.popen.007
anchor     functions/popen.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2024
says       With mode "r" or "w" (no second character) the descriptor
           under the stream has FD_CLOEXEC clear.
test       t/007-cloexec-clear.c

assertion  XSH.popen.008
anchor     functions/popen.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2024
says       With mode "re" or "we" the descriptor under the stream has
           FD_CLOEXEC set, and the stream works as before (a read or a
           write still transfers data).
test       t/008-cloexec-set.c

assertion  XSH.popen.009
anchor     functions/popen.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2024
says       The descriptor under the returned stream has FD_CLOFORK clear,
           for modes "r" and "w".
test       t/009-clofork-stream.c

assertion  XSH.popen.010
anchor     functions/popen.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2024
says       With mode "r", the command's standard output descriptor has
           FD_CLOFORK clear: a utility the shell starts by fork (the
           command "cat file; true") still has the pipe as its standard
           output and its output arrives on the stream.
note       Observing a descriptor flag in the command itself has no
           portable means; a forked descendant that lost the descriptor
           would show it.
test       t/010-clofork-child.c

assertion  XSH.popen.011
anchor     functions/popen.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/V2_chap02.html#2.5.2
class      disputed
says       Right after popen(), fwide(stream, 0) returns a negative value
           (the stream is byte-oriented), for both modes.
note       disputed (Q-64): the page says pipe streams are byte-oriented;
           XSH 2.5.2 says orientation begins undetermined and is fixed by
           the first operation. Under that reading fwide() returns 0 here
           and the stream becomes byte-oriented on first use. Both
           readings agree that a wide-oriented stream is wrong. A FAIL is
           logged as INSPECT.
test       t/011-byte-oriented.c

assertion  XSH.popen.012
anchor     functions/popen.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      1988
says       The parent and the command run independently: popen() returns
           while a command that waits for input ("cat > out") is still
           running, and the parent can then write to it and close it.
test       t/012-independent.c

assertion  XSH.popen.013
anchor     functions/popen.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      1988
says       A command started by a later popen() does not hold the streams of
           earlier ones: with "cat > out" open for writing and a second
           popen("cat", "w") started, pclose() of the first returns (its
           command sees end-of-file) while the second stream is still open.
test       t/013-earlier-streams.c

assertion  XSH.popen.014
anchor     functions/popen.html#ERRORS/EMFILE
class      required
since      2008
says       With {STREAM_MAX} streams open (stdin, stdout and stderr count)
           popen() returns a null pointer with errno EMFILE.
note       SD5-XSH-ERN-149 (Issue 7) made this a required error. NOTINUSE
           when {STREAM_MAX} is indeterminate. Q-37 is the same question
           for fopen(); here a failure for lack of descriptors is the same
           errno and also accepted. The failed call may have partly run
           (XSH 2.3), so nothing else is examined.
test       t/014-stream-max.c

assertion  XSH.popen.015
anchor     functions/popen.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/popen.html#RATIONALE
class      required
since      2024
says       The command is passed after "--": a command word that begins with
           a hyphen-minus or a plus sign runs as a command (executable
           files -pcdash and +pcplus found through PATH, each printing a
           line).
note       Austin Group defect 1440 (Issue 8).
test       t/015-dashdash.c

assertion  XSH.popen.016
anchor     functions/popen.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      1988
says       The command has the caller's working directory and environment
           variables and its standard error is the caller's descriptor 2
           (here a file): a command that tests a file in the working
           directory, an environment variable the caller set, and writes a
           line to standard error, behaves accordingly.
test       t/016-inherits.c

assertion  XSH.popen.017
anchor     functions/popen.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      1988
says       A descriptor of the caller with FD_CLOEXEC set is closed in the
           command, and one without it is open (a redirection from each).
test       t/017-cloexec-caller.c

assertion  XSH.popen.018
anchor     functions/popen.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2024
says       A descriptor of the caller with FD_CLOFORK set is closed in the
           command.
note       UNRESOLVED when <fcntl.h> lacks FD_CLOFORK.
test       t/018-clofork-caller.c
